+# Early decision verification, October 15, 2026
+
+The default rolling mode completed six fresh workflow checks. These are engineering checks, not a new benchmark or promotion result. No model or encoder was fitted.
+
+| Case | Policy | Diagnosis, seconds | Repair command done, seconds | Recovery verified, seconds | Probes / credits |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| orders | voi | 7.1 | 7.9 | 49.7 | 1 / 1 |
+| catalogue-db | dp | 6.4 | 7.2 | 45.1 | 1 / 1 |
+| payment-cpu | expected-sarsa | 10.4 | 14.1 | 27.7 | 1 / 2 |
+| carts-memory | two-step-sarsa | 15.2 | 16.0 | 40.1 | 1 / 2 |
+| payment-delay | voi | 94.2 | 95.4 | 109.4 | 5 / 10 |
+| healthy | voi | 94.1 | No repair | 109.2 | 2 / 4 |
+
+Times start at the injection request and include injection-command overhead. Initial healthy preparation is excluded. Recovery time includes the repair command and five consecutive successful checkouts. The healthy control has no injected fault and no repair.
+
+The path-delay case needed longer evidence and paid refreshes. The ten-credit and five-probe limits remained in force. Old observations were replaced in the model input rather than counted twice.
+
+Earlier failed engineering results are preserved. Revision r1 deferred the memory case after wasting its budget on unrelated status checks. Revision r2 deferred the path-delay case because older evidence could not be refreshed. Final revision r3 fixes those scheduling and billing problems without lowering the confidence or measurement conditions.
+
+A single case of each fault does not establish general accuracy. Saved-prefix checks also lack repeated status snapshots and start after fault settling, so their prefix durations must not be described as fresh incident detection times. The original 120-second benchmark remains unchanged.
