+# Continuous collection and early decisions
+
+The default live demo now collects continuously while Terminal 1 is running. An incident does not start a new 120-second timer that must finish before diagnosis. The controller checks newly available evidence and repairs an owned fault as soon as the declared support and confidence conditions pass.
+
+This is a separate experimental live protocol, `rolling_early_r3`. The original 120-second benchmark, encoder thresholds, model, checkpoints and reserved comparisons remain unchanged. Short-window performance is not a new benchmark promotion result.
+
+## Start the demo
+
+Use the same checkout and variables as before. Restart Terminal 1 and Terminal 2 after the old controller has stopped safely.
+
+```powershell
+# In each terminal
+Set-Location 'C:\Users\jacks\.codex\worktrees\live-agent-demo\Project1\team-graph-rca'
+$demoPython = 'C:\Users\jacks\OneDrive\Desktop\Project1\team-graph-rca\.venv\Scripts\python.exe'
+$artifacts = 'C:\Users\jacks\OneDrive\Desktop\Project1\team-graph-rca'
+
+# Terminal 1
+& $demoPython pipeline/monitor.py --agent-demo --artifacts $artifacts
+
+# Terminal 2
+& $demoPython pipeline/llm_chat.py --agent-demo --env-file "$artifacts/pipeline/.env"
+
+# Terminal 3
+& $demoPython pipeline/fault_injector.py --agent-demo
+```
+
+Wait for `Controller ready. Rolling collection is active.` before selecting an incident. Startup verifies container identities and checks twenty healthy checkouts. This is initial preparation before a fault is injected, not a delay added after every alert. Normal continuous operation reuses the recent healthy buffer. After a repair, collection restarts with a fresh baseline so repaired traffic does not mix with the old incident.
+
+An active physical experiment blocks this collector. Continuous checkout traffic would otherwise interfere with that experiment. The rolling monitor holds the shared lab lease until it stops. Use `stop` in chat, or:
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython scripts/live_demo.py stop
+```
+
+For the older fixed-window mode, explicitly add `--collection windowed` to Terminal 1. No change to the injector or chat is needed.
+
+## Collection and decision rules
+
+Two background workers collect checkout outcomes and container states/statistics. They keep a bounded 180-second buffer. Neo4j's demo projection is refreshed while the controller is idle. After an alert, snapshots include only requests and polls that started after that alert boundary. Earlier healthy observations cannot dilute or confirm the incident.
+
+Each snapshot has its actual start and end timestamps. A five-second observation is never padded or labelled as a 120-second capture. Separate `ShortIncident` and `ShortPacket` contracts retain the original evidence field checks while scaling container sample support to the actual measured duration. The frozen benchmark contracts still require their original 120-second evidence and sample support.
+
+The graph receives the new snapshot, retrieves anomaly context and exposes only ready evidence for paid probes. Direct stopped-resource, CPU and retained-memory alerts are checked before nonspecific downstream checkout symptoms. Unrelated healthy status probes are excluded from the early action mask. This prevents spending the budget on them while relevant evidence is still warming up.
+
+| Evidence | Earliest allowed use |
+| --- | --- |
+| Container status | Two matching post-alert observations, latest no more than six seconds old |
+| CPU | At least ten seconds, adequate sample coverage and bounded gaps |
+| Retained memory pressure | At least fifteen seconds with adequate coverage |
+| Memory normality or growth | At least ninety seconds and ninety seconds of actual sample span |
+| Checkout latency/errors | At least fifteen seconds and twenty completed observations of the checkout operation |
+| Logs | Unavailable in this short-window protocol; use fixed-window mode when logs are required |
+
+These are readiness conditions, not promised diagnosis times. A slow checkout may take much longer to produce twenty observations. Ambiguous classifications can need additional evidence or end in deferral. The readiness mask differs from training, so tabular methods can reach unseen states and use their existing initialization or declared hybrid fallback. The live results are not proof that the frozen SARSA policies improved on their benchmark.
+
+One investigation has one persistent budget of ten credits and five probe acquisitions. Waiting does not reset the budget. A terminal policy proposal does not cause an early repair unless the shared classifier reaches at least 90% model confidence and purchased evidence supports the mechanism. Model confidence is not a calibrated guarantee of correctness.
+
+If evidence used in a proposed decision is more than fifteen seconds old, the controller must obtain a current packet. That refresh costs the tool's credits again and uses another probe acquisition. The model sees the latest symbol for that tool, not repeated copies of its likelihood. The full acquisition audit keeps the earlier packet and its cost. If fresh support cannot be obtained within the budget, the agent defers.
+
+At most 120 seconds of post-alert observation is allowed. The controller may defer earlier if the budget is exhausted. Deferral triggers cleanup of the injector's own fault, which is reported separately from successful agent repair. No confidence condition or original promotion gate is lowered to force a pass.
+
+## What timing means
+
+Each receipt separates:
+
+- `injection_command_seconds`: time spent applying the demo condition.
+- `diagnosis_seconds`: elapsed time from the injection request to the decision.
+- `observation_seconds`: the actual measured post-alert window used by the decision.
+- `repair_started_seconds`: when the controller started the owned repair.
+- `repair_command_completed_seconds`: when that command returned.
+- `verified_recovery_seconds`: when independent recovery checks passed.
+
+Starting a stopped Java service can take longer than identifying it. The controller still requires five consecutive semantic checkout successes, running resource containers and zero proxy delay before reporting recovery. Retained memory must also release near the measured baseline.
+
+## Tests and limits
+
+Unit tests cover short timestamps, coverage and sample counts, cross-boundary request/poll exclusion, repeated status confirmation, stale evidence, paid refresh, unique model inputs, the persistent budget and cleanup on failure.
+
+`scripts/verify_early_prefixes.py` assesses saved validation capture prefixes offline. Those captures were collected after fault settling, so a ten-second prefix is not proof of ten-second detection from a new real fault. The saved captures also have only one status snapshot. They cannot establish the new two-observation stop-confirmation condition, and the test deliberately defers rather than inventing another snapshot.
+
+Fresh runs and timing receipts are kept separately under `runs/early_live_orders` and `runs/early_live_sweep`. Revision r1's SARSA memory deferral is preserved. It exposed premature spending on unrelated running-status probes. Revision r2 introduced relevance scheduling and stricter post-alert sampling. Revision r3 adds paid refresh for older evidence. None of these revisions retrains from validation or reserved data.
+
+See `early_decision_verification.json` for the final checks actually completed. A readiness target or unit-test timestamp must not be presented as measured live repair latency.
