+# Live agent demo
+
+The default now uses continuous collection and early decisions. See early_decisions.md for the current timing rules and launch commands. The fixed-window timings below apply only when Terminal 1 uses --collection windowed.
+
+The monitor, chat and injector now share one controller. VOI, DP, Expected SARSA or Two-step SARSA chooses diagnostic probes. The shared classifier predicts the cause. The chat window explains the result. A separate controller restores an owned demo fault and tests whether the checkout works again.
+
+This mode supports the five measured v2 faults and a healthy control. Broader v3 faults and simultaneous faults require the separate v3 model. The menu does not offer them here.
+
+Groq narration is enabled at the user's request. It receives only diagnosis, confidence, status and repair/health outcomes. Raw logs, incident IDs, injector labels and checkpoint contents stay local. The key is in the original repository's ignored pipeline/.env file.
+
+## Before starting
+
+Docker Desktop must be running. Use the existing `rca-lab` project, with its pinned helper, payment proxy, container identities and resource limits. Do not rebuild the lab or run the original fault menu alongside this demo. Another experiment controller must finish and release its lab lease first. The demo rejects an occupied lab, including the gaps between v3 pilot incidents.
+
+For this isolated checkout, open three PowerShell terminals. In **each** terminal run:
+
+```powershell
+Set-Location 'C:\Users\jacks\.codex\worktrees\live-agent-demo\Project1\team-graph-rca'
+$demoPython = 'C:\Users\jacks\OneDrive\Desktop\Project1\team-graph-rca\.venv\Scripts\python.exe'
+$artifacts = 'C:\Users\jacks\OneDrive\Desktop\Project1\team-graph-rca'
+```
+
+GraphRAG integration is described in graph_live_integration.md. Neo4j is required for --agent-demo. The source is on branch `codex/live-agent-demo`, based on the committed v2 work. Models and checkpoints stay in the original repository. There is no training during the demo.
+
+The entry points verify and materialize the committed v2 source snapshot under `runs/live_demo_runtime`. That keeps the trained policies separate from an editable v3 package, even after these adapters are copied or cherry-picked onto a branch containing v3. The snapshot, topology, model, encoder, task, dependency lock and checkpoints must all match their saved identities. A failed identity check requires investigation rather than rebuilding or bypassing the check.
+
+## Terminal 1, monitor and controller
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython pipeline/monitor.py --agent-demo --artifacts $artifacts
+```
+
+Wait for the `ready` event. This process is the only owner of the diagnostic workflow and Docker recovery commands. It collects a healthy baseline when the injector requests a case, then collects the incident window. The initial policy is VOI.
+
+## Terminal 2, chat output
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython pipeline/llm_chat.py --agent-demo --env-file "$artifacts/pipeline/.env"
+```
+
+Use these commands at `chat>`:
+
+```text
+policy voi
+policy dp
+policy expected-sarsa
+policy two-step-sarsa
+status
+stop
+quit
+```
+
+Wait for the policy acknowledgement before requesting a case. A change applies to the next incident. `stop` asks Terminal 1 to finish the current incident, recover its fault and stop at the safe boundary. `quit` closes only the chat viewer.
+
+The chat displays each selected tool, its measured response, accumulated credits and decision time. The final plain-language message distinguishes agent repair from injector cleanup. A wrong diagnosis can lead to cleanup without a successful agent repair.
+
+## Terminal 3, injector menu
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython pipeline/fault_injector.py --agent-demo
+```
+
+Choose one case and let it finish before requesting another:
+
+| Case | Injected condition | Bounded repair |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| healthy | No injected fault | No repair, verify checkout health |
+| catalogue-db | Stop the catalogue database | Start that same container |
+| orders | Stop orders | Start that same container |
+| payment-cpu | Run the leased CPU helper in payment | Stop only that helper lease |
+| payment-delay | Add 800 ms on orders to payment | Reset the owned proxy delay |
+| carts-memory | Retain memory near the registered pressure range | Release only that helper lease |
+
+For a single request without the menu:
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython pipeline/fault_injector.py --agent-demo --case payment-cpu
+```
+
+The controller checks 120 seconds of healthy checkout traffic, injects the chosen condition, settles for 30 seconds and collects a fixed 120-second incident window. Telemetry gets a 10-second ingestion allowance. Expect about five minutes per case, plus recovery time.
+
+The policies buy evidence from that just-collected window. They do not each wait for a new two-minute window per probe. This is a live-window demonstration using the same evidence contract as training. The policy receives neither the injector's case nor its private recovery journal. Collection remains separate from the policy's paid evidence ledger.
+
+The policy can use status, payment CPU, carts memory, checkout error and latency measurements, and bounded logs. The existing collectors and encoders implement those tools. The new adapter adds paid dispatch, trace events, ownership checks and controlled repair. Ten credits and five probes remain the registered limits.
+
+Repair needs an owned fault, a matching diagnosis with at least 80% model confidence, and paid evidence of its mechanism. That confidence rule is a demo repair rule, not a changed benchmark promotion gate. The controller also verifies exact container and helper identities. An agent never executes arbitrary shell text. The language wrapper has no executable tools.
+
+## What counts as success
+
+The controller reports agent repair only when the agent's evidence justified the matching owned command and the fault had not already expired. It then needs five consecutive semantic checkout successes, all resource containers running and zero proxy delay. For a memory injection it also checks that the retained allocation released near the measured baseline.
+
+These are functional recovery checks for controlled lab injections. They are not a new accuracy benchmark or proof of recovery from arbitrary production faults. The classifier's posterior confidence is not a guarantee of correctness. Expected SARSA and Two-step SARSA keep their existing v2 limitations.
+
+Each run writes `baseline.json`, `window.json`, `diagnosis.json`, `recovery.json` when a fault was injected, and `receipt.json` under `runs/live_demo/incidents/<id>`. `events.jsonl` is the public event stream. `narration/<id>.json` records the displayed summary. These files never enter the training or reserved comparison datasets.
+
+## Stop and recover
+
+Normal shutdown:
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython scripts/live_demo.py stop
+```
+
+Or press Ctrl+C in Terminal 1. The controller's cleanup path restores its own injection and checks recovery before releasing the lab. Do not force-close Terminal 1 or manually remove a shared lab lock.
+
+If the process was killed or the laptop restarted, use:
+
+```powershell
+& $demoPython scripts/live_demo.py recover --artifacts $artifacts
+```
+
+Recovery rejects a still-running owner, a foreign experiment marker, another controller's lease, a changed container identity and a helper with a different lease. It uses the saved journal, verifies health and only then removes its own recovery marker and stale lock. If recovery fails, keep the marker and inspect the saved receipt.
+
+Without `--agent-demo`, the three original scripts retain their legacy GraphRAG workflow. The live demo requires Neo4j for dependencies, anomaly retrieval and purchased evidence. The language model explains the result; the agent and classifier perform diagnosis.
+
+## Verification
+
+`scripts/verify_live_adapter.py --artifacts $artifacts` checks six saved validation cases with all four policies. It compares packet contents, probe order, credits and decisions with the frozen replay. It reads no reserved responses, changes no encoder or checkpoint and injects no physical fault.
+
+`scripts/verify_live_bus.py --artifacts $artifacts` starts all three actual terminal wrappers while a foreign lab lease is present. It checks that the request is rejected, the chat explains the failure and the foreign lease remains unchanged.
+
+`scripts/verify_live_workflow.py --artifacts $artifacts --wait-for-lab` waits for an exclusive lab slot and runs six fresh VOI cases plus fresh DP, Expected SARSA and Two-step SARSA cases. Its physical receipts are under `runs/live_demo_verification/physical`. A pending or failed check must not be presented as passed.
+
+The optional HTTP adapters follow the official [Gemini generation API](https://ai.google.dev/api/generate-content) and [Groq API endpoint](https://console.groq.com/docs/openai). They receive only the fields in `live_demo_narration_payload.example.json`. Use --no-llm to select local narration. A provider failure falls back to the local summary.
